<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Rus' Khaganate</h1><p class="st-meta">830 CE – 882 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A shadowy 9th-century polity of Scandinavian Rus' whose ruler took the title khagan, predecessor of the state of Kievan Rus'.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 839 envoys of a people called the Rhos, whose ruler was styled chacanus, reached the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> with a Byzantine embassy and turned out to be Swedes. Historians use the term Rus' Khaganate for this polity of Scandinavian traders and warriors in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, but its location, perhaps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staraya-ladoga">Ladoga</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Novgorod</a> region, is debated.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Arab geographers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-rusta">Ibn Rusta</a> describe the Rus' as living on a marshy island under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>, collecting slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and selling them for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. The title was borrowed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> and claimed equal rank with their ruler. The Rus' attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 860 may have been launched from this state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Its fate is unclear. The chronicle tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik">Rurik's</a> arrival around 862 and Oleg's capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> in 882 describes the creation of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, into which the earlier khaganate was presumably absorbed. Later Kievan rulers such as Vladimir I were still occasionally called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="khanate, 830 CE – 882 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 74 V58 Q50 30 82 58 V74 Z"/><path d="M42 74 V60 H58 V74"/><path d="M30 50 L50 36 L70 50"/><path d="M50 36 V26"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">khanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:76.22%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>830 CE – 882 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Russia, Ukraine</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rhos, Russian Khaganate</dd></dl></aside></div>
